 311: /* ================================================================== row-level security + grants == */
 312: 
 313: alter table public.accounts enable row level security;
 314: alter table public.organizations enable row level security;
 315: alter table public.org_memberships enable row level security;
 316: alter table public.acknowledgments enable row level security;
 317: 
 318: -- NO POLICIES ARE ADDED. With row-level security on and no policy, every client-key read and write
 319: -- is denied, which is exactly what this leaf's tests need and the safe default for everything else.
 320: -- The tenant-isolation policy set â€” who may read what, across NGOs, volunteers and the platform
 321: -- administrator â€” is AT-001.21 through .24 and .40, and belongs to the tenant-isolation
 322: -- deliverable.
 323: 
 324: -- WHY THIS GRANT EXISTS, because it looks backwards on first reading.
 325: --
 326: -- `[api] auto_expose_new_tables` is unset in `supabase/config.toml`, so new tables are NOT reachable
 327: -- through the Data API roles without an explicit grant. Left ungranted, a client-key insert into
 328: -- `public.accounts` fails at the PRIVILEGE layer ("permission denied for table accounts") and
 329: -- row-level security is never consulted at all â€” so "denied by row-level security" would be a claim
 330: -- about a mechanism that did not run.
 331: --
 332: -- Granting the privilege and denying by policy is the canonical Supabase posture and it makes the
 333: -- claim true: with the grant present and no insert policy, the refusal is
 334: -- "new row violates row-level security policy for table \"accounts\"", which names the layer that
 335: -- actually refused. `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.ts` asserts that message, not merely that
 336: -- something failed. The other three tables get no grant and are unreachable through the Data API
 337: -- entirely, which is stricter still.
 338: grant select, insert on public.accounts to authenticated;
 339: 
 340: -- THE ONE PRIVILEGE THE SERVICE ROLE HOLDS ON A TABLE, and it is a read.
 341: --
 342: -- `create-organization` asks the Data API for the caller's `account_type` before deciding, and that
 343: -- read is the only thing on either edge-function path that does not go through one of the functions
 344: -- above. Everything that WRITES goes through a SECURITY DEFINER function, so the service role has
 345: -- no INSERT anywhere in this schema â€” which is what stops the service-role key from writing
 346: -- `account_type = 'platform_admin'` directly past `complete_signup`'s refusal.
 347: --
 348: -- A consequence worth stating rather than discovering later: a platform administrator therefore
 349: -- cannot be provisioned with the service-role key either. Provisioning one is a direct database
 350: -- operation by an operator. That is a narrower authority than the service role, not a wider one,
 351: -- and it keeps "provisioned, never self-signed-up" (AT-001.07) true of every path a running service
 352: -- has access to.
 353: grant select on public.accounts to service_role;
 354: 
 355: -- THE REVOKE IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF, AND IT WAS FOUND BY MEASURING RATHER THAN BY REASONING.
 356: --
 357: -- PostgreSQL grants EXECUTE on a new function to PUBLIC by default, and every Data API role
 358: -- inherits that. The first replay of this migration was introspected with
 359: -- `has_function_privilege`, and `anon` could execute BOTH functions â€” so an anonymous caller
 360: -- holding only the publishable key could have POSTed `/rest/v1/rpc/complete_signup` with any
 361: -- `p_account_id` at all and minted an `ngo` account and an organisation against someone else's auth
 362: -- user. (The `platform_admin` guard above held, so the worst case was not an administrator; it was
 363: -- still a write nobody authorised.) Granting without revoking would have left that open while the
 364: -- migration read as if it had locked the functions down.
 365: --
 366: -- Both functions are server-side only: the edge functions call them with the service role, and no
 367: -- browser has any business calling either. `has_platform_acknowledgment` is restricted just as
 368: -- tightly â€” it answers a question about an account id, and letting any signed-in caller ask it
 369: -- about any id is a small existence oracle for no gain, since nothing client-side calls it.
 370: revoke execute on function public.complete_signup(uuid, text, text, text, inet) from public;
 371: revoke execute on function public.create_organization(uuid, text) from public;
 372: revoke execute on function public.has_platform_acknowledgment(uuid) from public;
 373: 
 374: grant execute on function public.complete_signup(uuid, text, text, text, inet) to service_role;
 375: grant execute on function public.create_organization(uuid, text) to service_role;
 376: grant execute on function public.has_platform_acknowledgment(uuid) to service_role;
 377: 
 378: -- PostgREST caches the schema. Without this, the first call to a freshly created function is a 404
 379: -- from the schema cache rather than a real answer.
 380: notify pgrst, 'reload schema';

